# The @CALCULUS@ calculus

What this checker checks proofs in. This document is yours to fill in as the
calculus takes shape; what follows is the frame.

## The signature

`install/defs/@CALCULUS@.eo`, in [Eunoia](https://github.com/cvc5/ethos), the
logical framework of the Ethos proof checker. It declares the sorts, the
operators and the proof rules.

> Describe the calculus here: what it is for, which theories it covers, and
> what a proof in it looks like.

## What its symbols mean

`install/defs/@CALCULUS@.eos` maps each symbol into the deep embedding of
SMT-LIB, and `install/defs/smt.eos` says what that target means to a model.
Together they are what makes a verdict of `correct` an assertion about
satisfiability rather than about a syntactic derivation.

## The signature contract

The checker layer depends on the signature in four places. All four are checked
by `install/install-@CALCLOWER@.sh` against the compiler's output, before
anything is installed.

**Always required:**

| requirement | why |
| ----------- | --- |
| a binary operator **`and`** | the soundness statement is about the conjunction of a proof's assumptions, and the checker folds its proof stack with it |
| **`and` sent to `SmtTerm.and`** | a signature that declared `and` and translated it elsewhere would still compile and still check proofs, with the conclusion about the wrong formula. Nothing downstream re-checks this |
| **`true`** / **`false`** | `false` is the refutation target, `true` terminates the assumption chain. Eunoia builtins, so nothing declares them |

**Required only if rules gather `:list` premises with `and`:**

| requirement | why |
| ----------- | --- |
| `and` declared **`:right-assoc-nil true`** | such a rule builds its premise list through `__eo_nil`, and the arm returning `true` for `and` exists only because of the attribute |

The second is not a core requirement: with a plain binary `and` the assumption
chain, the refutation test and the SMT translation are unchanged, and only the
surface syntax for n-ary `(and a b c)` is lost.

For example, in `install/defs/@CALCULUS@.eo`:

```
(declare-const and (-> Bool Bool Bool) :right-assoc-nil true)
```

and in `install/defs/@CALCULUS@.eos`, a rule sending it to the SMT-LIB `and`.

Everything else in the vocabulary is yours. `not`, `=` and `imp` are **not**
required by the checker — they are used only by rule proofs, so a calculus
without them costs nothing.

## The profile

`install/defs/profile.conf` records the high-level facts about @CALCULUS@ that
this checker is documented against. `install-@CALCLOWER@.sh` re-checks the ones
the compiled output can settle.

| question | flag | kind |
| -------- | ---- | ---- |
| Do rules discharge assumptions (`scope`, compiling to step-pop)? | `--[no-]scopes` | **derived** — the step-pop dispatch arms are emitted per rule |
| Do rules gather `:list` premises? | `--[no-]list-premises` | **derived** — the premise-list calls are emitted per rule, and the nil for the gathering operator either exists or does not |
| Is `smt.eos` Logos's SMT-LIB semantics, unmodified? | computed | **derived** — by digest |
| How many indices do operators take, at most? | `--indexed-ops N` | **derived** — an unused arity still gets an enum, but holding a placeholder `\| None`, so a real constructor is the signal |
| Should the generated parser be installed? | `--[no-]parser` | **derived**, and the only entry that is also a *choice*: `--no-parser` really does change what is installed |
| Does the calculus have algebraic datatypes? | `--[no-]datatypes` | **declared** — eoc emits the datatype machinery for every signature |
| Are any rules binder-sensitive? | `--[no-]binders` | **declared** — likewise unconditional, and a binder in the signature does not imply a rule reasoning under one |
| Does the semantics lean on a total order on values? | `--[no-]value-ordering` | **declared** — eoc emits the same `SmtValueOrder` either way |

**None of these trims anything.** What this package contains is decided by
`install/defs/@CALCULUS@.eo` and by the eoc compiler; the profile describes the
calculus rather than configuring it. The declared entries are declared because
the machinery they name is emitted by eoc unconditionally, so nothing in the
output tells the answers apart.

Keep it accurate as the calculus grows: it is what the documentation here
assumes.

## Cross-checking with ethos

`install/get-eo-compiler.sh` also builds ethos, the reference checker for
Eunoia. It reads `install/defs/@CALCULUS@.eo` directly, so it works before any
of the Lean development does:

```bash
scripts/check-with-ethos.sh
```

Ethos is unverified, so agreement proves nothing. It is a fast, independent
implementation of the same signature, which makes it good at catching a
compiled calculus that has drifted from the signature it came from.

## The rules

One file per rule under `@CALCULUS@/Proofs/Rules/`, each with the rule's
correctness lemma and its proof. `@CALCULUS@/Proofs/RuleLemmas.lean` is the
dispatcher over them.

> As rules are added, this is where to say which are trusted, which are proven,
> and which are still stubs.

## Proof syntax

Proofs are s-expressions in the syntax Ethos accepts:

```
(declare-const x Int)
(assume @p0 (= y x))
(step @p1 :rule symm :premises (@p0))
```

The commands and term syntax the parser accepts follow from
`@CALCULUS@/Parser.lean`, which is generated from the signature.
